@startuml example-perplexity-eval
' How Perplexity::corpus_perplexity turns a test corpus into PP + OOV statistics:
' a single streaming pass, one (word | context) query per token, accumulation in
' log space, and exactly one exp() at the end. Colour: corpus / readers (grey),
' statistical LM core (blue), accumulator & application (slate), result (teal).
' Render: plantuml -tsvg docs/diagrams/example-perplexity-eval.puml
' Source of truth for Figure 1 in docs/examples/perplexity-scoring.md §3
skinparam backgroundColor #FAFAFA
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultTextAlignment center
skinparam linetype ortho
skinparam rectangle {
  FontColor white
  FontStyle bold
  BorderColor #263238
  roundCorner 12
}
skinparam ArrowColor #37474F
skinparam ArrowFontColor #37474F
title Perplexity — one streaming pass · log-space accumulation · a single exp

rectangle "**test corpus**\nheld out — never seen in training" as corpus #546E7A
rectangle "**reader.sentences()**\nlowercased · split on [.!?]+␣\n(min 10 characters)" as sent #546E7A
rectangle "**sentence.split_whitespace()**\ntokens w₁ … w_N\npunctuation is **not** stripped here — §5" as tok #546E7A

rectangle "**context window**\nhᵢ = the ≤ n−1 tokens preceding wᵢ\n(clamped at the sentence start)" as ctx #1565C0
rectangle "**model.log_prob(wᵢ, hᵢ)**\nModified Kneser-Ney backoff\nalways finite, always < 0" as lp #1565C0

rectangle "**accumulate**\nΣ ← Σ + log ℙ(wᵢ | hᵢ)\nN ← N + 1\nOOV++ if log ℙ ≤ log(1/|V|)" as acc #455A64
rectangle "**PerplexityResult**\nperplexity = exp(−Σ / N)\noov_count · oov_rate · total_tokens\ntotal_log_prob · sentence_count" as res #00838F

corpus --> sent
sent --> tok
tok --> ctx : for i = 1 … N
ctx --> lp
lp --> acc
acc --> ctx : next token
acc --> res : after the last token

note right of lp #E3F2FD
  The recursion terminates in the
  strictly-positive unigram term 1/|V|,
  so ℙ > 0 always, log ℙ never reaches
  −∞, and PP is always finite. The
  backoff weight λ(h) < 1 scales that
  term down — which is why the OOV
  test below is ≤, not =.
end note

note bottom of acc #ECEFF1
  Summing logs (never multiplying
  probabilities) is what keeps a
  10⁶-token corpus from underflowing.
end note

@enduml
